---
// PDF and EPUB export: checked against the export, theming, syntax, and
// frontmatter docs, PageActions.astro (the Export menu and the EPUB
// rewrite), RootLayout.astro (where the rail renders), the print rules in
// theme/entry.ts, and epub-gen-memory 1.1.2's browser bundle. Ran headless
// Chromium (Playwright) against the docs dev server: printed
// /docs/content/syntax and /docs/configuration/export to PDF in light and
// dark themes, downloaded real EPUBs from the Export menu and unzipped them,
// injected images, a long code line, and a closed accordion to test those
// cases, and applied the theme.css print rules below as an injected style
// tag. Rendered the test page's diagram with the pinned mermaid-cli
// command. Browser dialog labels come from the Chromium and Firefox string
// files; WebP's status from the EPUB 3.2 and 3.3 specs. Not yet run end to
// end on a fresh project (the test page itself was not built), and the EPUBs
// were not opened in Apple Books, calibre, or an e-ink reader.
// Updated for the print and EPUB fixes on main (e12b668a, 2dda5feb):
// the print block in theme/entry.ts, the beforeprint switch in
// head-scripts.ts, and the EPUB metadata, link, and image handling in
// PageActions.astro. Read in the source; not rerun in a browser.
// Updated for the page actions in the mobile On this page menu (2c113598):
// RootLayout.astro, TableOfContents.astro, and PageActions.astro. Opened the
// menu on a probe site in Chrome at 390, 768, and 1,100 pixels, and ran
// Export to PDF and Export to EPUB from it before and after a client-side
// navigation.
import Callout from "blume/components/content/Callout.astro";
import CodeBlock from "blume/components/content/CodeBlock.astro";
import GuidePage from "../_guides/GuidePage.astro";
import { guideById } from "../_guides/guides.ts";
---

<GuidePage guide={guideById("documentation-pdf-epub-export")}>
  <p>
    Readers can save a docs page for offline use once you turn on Blume's{" "}
    <code>export</code> option. Every docs page then gets an{" "}
    <strong>Export</strong> action under its table of contents, with{" "}
    <strong>Export to PDF</strong> and <strong>Export to EPUB</strong>. PDF
    goes through the browser's print dialog, with a print stylesheet that
    strips the site down to the article. EPUB is generated in the reader's
    browser and downloads as a <code>.epub</code> file for Apple Books,
    calibre, or an e-reader. Neither needs a server, so a static site stays
    static.
  </p>
  <p>
    Both formats export the one page the reader is on, not your documentation
    as a book. If you need a whole manual with a cover and chapters, that's a
    publishing pipeline Blume doesn't provide. For PDF alone, you may not need
    the option: the print stylesheet ships on every Blume site, so a reader who
    presses Ctrl+P (Cmd+P on a Mac) already gets the stripped-down page.{" "}
    <code>export</code> adds a menu entry that opens the print dialog, plus
    the EPUB generator.
  </p>
  <p>
    This guide turns the action on, builds a test page with the content that
    exports worst, checks both files against a checklist, and fixes what
    doesn't carry over.
  </p>

  <h2>Turn on the Export action</h2>
  <p>Add <code>export</code> to your config. <code>true</code> turns on both formats:</p>
  <CodeBlock
    code={`import { defineConfig } from "blume";

export default defineConfig({
  title: "Acme Docs",
  export: true,
});`}
    lang="ts"
    title="blume.config.ts"
  />
  <p>
    To offer one format, pass an object instead. A format you leave out stays
    off, so this shows only the PDF entry:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={`export: { pdf: true },`} lang="ts" title="blume.config.ts" />
  <p>
    The action sits below the table of contents. Where that is depends on the
    window and the page:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      In a window at least 1,280 pixels wide, it's in the right-hand rail. In
      a narrower window or on a phone, it's at the bottom of the{" "}
      <strong>On this page</strong> menu above the content, and its PDF and
      EPUB entries open right under it.
    </li>
    <li>
      Pages whose <a href="/docs/content/frontmatter#layout">layout mode</a>{" "}
      drops the table of contents (<code>wide</code>, <code>center</code>,{" "}
      <code>frame</code>, and <code>custom</code>) have no rail and no menu,
      and API operation pages have neither.
    </li>
    <li>
      Turning the outline off with <code>toc: false</code> keeps both, so the
      action stays. The menu then holds only the page actions.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Build a test page</h2>
  <p>
    Exports go wrong on particular content, so test with a page that has all
    of it: a long command, a titled code block, a collapsed code block, tabs, a
    diagram, an image, a callout, and an accordion. Save this as{" "}
    <code>docs/export-test.mdx</code>, and put any screenshot at{" "}
    <code>docs/images/dashboard.png</code>:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    code={`---
title: Send your first message
description: Install the Acme SDK, send a test SMS, and check that it arrived.
---

Install the SDK with the package manager your project uses:

\`\`\`package-install
npm i @acme/messages
\`\`\`

## Set your API key

<Tabs>
  <Tab title="macOS and Linux">
    On macOS or Linux, export the key in your shell:

    \`\`\`bash
    export ACME_KEY="sk_test_123"
    \`\`\`

  </Tab>
  <Tab title="Windows">
    On Windows, set the key in PowerShell:

    \`\`\`powershell
    $env:ACME_KEY = "sk_test_123"
    \`\`\`

  </Tab>
</Tabs>

## Send a message

Send a test SMS from the command line:

\`\`\`bash
curl -X POST https://api.acme.example/v1/messages -H "Authorization: Bearer $ACME_KEY" -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '{"to":"+15550100","channel":"sms","template":"welcome"}'
\`\`\`

Or from your code:

\`\`\`ts send.ts
import { Acme } from "@acme/messages";

const acme = new Acme({ apiKey: process.env.ACME_KEY });
const message = await acme.messages.send({
  to: "+15550100",
  channel: "sms",
  template: "welcome",
});
console.log(message.id, message.status);
\`\`\`

The API answers with the new message:

\`\`\`json response.json expandable
{
  "id": "msg_01J9ZKQ4",
  "object": "message",
  "channel": "sms",
  "to": "+15550100",
  "template": "welcome",
  "status": "queued",
  "created_at": "2026-09-27T10:00:00Z",
  "updated_at": "2026-09-27T10:00:00Z",
  "segments": 1,
  "error": null,
  "metadata": {
    "source": "quickstart"
  },
  "links": {
    "self": "https://api.acme.example/v1/messages/msg_01J9ZKQ4"
  }
}
\`\`\`

## Check delivery status

\`\`\`mermaid
sequenceDiagram
  participant App
  participant API as Acme API
  participant Phone
  App->>API: POST /messages
  API-->>App: 202, status queued
  API->>Phone: SMS
  App->>API: GET /messages/msg_01J9ZKQ4
  API-->>App: status delivered
\`\`\`

![The Acme dashboard with the test message marked Delivered](./images/dashboard.png)

| Status      | Meaning                        |
| ----------- | ------------------------------ |
| \`queued\`    | Accepted, not yet sent         |
| \`sent\`      | Handed to the carrier          |
| \`delivered\` | The carrier confirmed delivery |
| \`failed\`    | See the \`error\` field          |

:::warning
Test keys only send to phone numbers you've verified in the dashboard.
:::

<Accordion>
  <AccordionItem title="Why is my message still queued?">
    A message to a new number can wait a few minutes for carrier checks.
    Resend it from [Send a message](#send-a-message), or go back to
    [the overview](/).
  </AccordionItem>
</Accordion>`}
    lang="mdx"
    title="docs/export-test.mdx"
  />
  <p>
    Run <code>npx blume dev</code> and open <code>/export-test</code>. Wait
    for the diagram to draw before you export. Mermaid renders in the browser after the page loads, and a diagram
    that hasn't finished prints as a blank space.
  </p>

  <h2>Check the PDF</h2>
  <p>
    You can export from either theme. When a reader prints from the dark
    theme, Blume switches the page to the light theme for the printout and
    back again afterward, so the PDF has dark text on white paper.
  </p>
  <p>
    Choose <strong>Export</strong>, then <strong>Export to PDF</strong>. In
    Chrome, pick <strong>Save as PDF</strong> as the destination, open{" "}
    <strong>More settings</strong>, and turn on{" "}
    <strong>Background graphics</strong> so the callout keeps its fill. In
    Firefox, the destination is <strong>Save to PDF</strong> and the option is{" "}
    <strong>Print backgrounds</strong>. Other browsers word it differently.
  </p>
  <p>
    The header, sidebar, rail, and breadcrumbs are gone, and so is everything
    after the article: feedback, "Last updated", previous and next links, and
    the site footer. Code blocks print without their buttons, and{" "}
    <code>response.json</code> prints in full even though it's collapsed on
    screen. Links stay clickable, and in Chrome they point at full URLs on
    your site. Compare the rest against the{" "}
    <a href="#export-checklist">checklist</a> below. On this page, expect two
    problems: the <code>curl</code> command is cut off at the right margin,
    and the accordion prints only its title.
  </p>

  <h2>Check the EPUB</h2>
  <p>
    Choose <strong>Export</strong>, then <strong>Export to EPUB</strong>. The
    entry reads <strong>Generating…</strong> while Blume loads the generator
    and downloads the page's images, then the browser saves{" "}
    <code>export-test.epub</code>. The file is named after the page's path,
    with slashes turned into hyphens, so <code>/guides/setup</code> becomes{" "}
    <code>guides-setup.epub</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    None of the site's CSS or JavaScript goes into the file. Blume rewrites the
    article as plain HTML with a small built-in e-reader stylesheet, and the
    result is one chapter:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>The book's title is the page's title.</li>
    <li>It opens on a contents page with a single entry, then the page.</li>
    <li>
      The author and publisher are your site's <code>title</code>, and the
      language is the page's language, so a translated page exports as a book
      in that language.
    </li>
    <li>
      Links to other pages become full URLs on your site, since the book holds
      only this page.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Open the file in Apple Books and in calibre's E-book viewer, and on an
    e-reader if your readers use one. Reading apps lay out the same file
    differently, so check each one against the checklist.
  </p>

  <h2>Export checklist</h2>
  <p>
    What happens to each part of the test page. The PDF column comes from
    Chrome's print output, and the EPUB column from the file's contents.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th scope="col">On the page</th>
        <th scope="col">In the PDF</th>
        <th scope="col">In the EPUB</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Header, sidebar, rail, breadcrumbs</td>
        <td>Removed</td>
        <td>Not included</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Feedback, "Last updated", previous and next links, site footer</td>
        <td>Removed</td>
        <td>Not included</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Code highlighting</td>
        <td>Kept</td>
        <td>Plain monospace</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Long code lines</td>
        <td>Cut off at the margin</td>
        <td>Wrapped</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Code block titles, like <code>send.ts</code></td>
        <td>Printed</td>
        <td>Dropped</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>expandable</code> code blocks</td>
        <td>Printed in full</td>
        <td>Complete</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Copy buttons and icons</td>
        <td>Buttons removed, icons printed</td>
        <td>Dropped</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Tabs, including package installs</td>
        <td>Every panel, under the tab strip</td>
        <td>Every panel, with no tab labels</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Accordions</td>
        <td>Title only, unless open when printed</td>
        <td>Title and body; the reader decides how to show them</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Mermaid diagrams</td>
        <td>Printed, once rendered</td>
        <td>Dropped</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Images</td>
        <td>Printed</td>
        <td>Downloaded into the file</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Links to other pages</td>
        <td>Full URLs to your site</td>
        <td>Full URLs to your site</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Two EPUB rows need a closer look in each app. Blume converts colocated
    images to WebP, which EPUB only made a core image format in version 3.3,
    so check that images show on the oldest reader you care about. And an
    image without alt text gets the alt text "image-placeholder" in the file.
  </p>

  <h2>Adjust the page for readable exports</h2>
  <p>
    Most fixes are to the content, and they make the web page clearer too.
  </p>

  <h3>Wrap long code lines</h3>
  <p>
    Paper can't scroll, so wrap long lines. Append <code>wrap</code> to one
    fence (<code>```bash wrap</code>), or wrap every block on the site:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    code={`markdown: {
  code: { wrap: true },
},`}
    lang="ts"
    title="blume.config.ts"
  />
  <p>
    Both wrap on screen too. The print rules further down wrap only on paper.
    See <a href="/docs/content/syntax#long-lines-and-long-blocks">Long lines
    and long blocks</a> for the other fence options.
  </p>

  <h3>Say in the text what the chrome said</h3>
  <p>
    The EPUB drops code block titles and tab labels, so don't let them carry
    information alone. Name the file in the sentence before the block
    ("Or from your code, in <code>send.ts</code>:"), and name the platform
    inside each tab panel. The test page's tabs already do this, which is why
    they still read correctly without labels.
  </p>

  <h3>Give every diagram a text fallback</h3>
  <p>
    A Mermaid diagram draws itself with JavaScript, so it never reaches the
    EPUB. Add a sentence after it that states what it shows. When a diagram
    has to survive in both formats, render it to an image and reference that
    instead. Move the diagram source into <code>diagrams/delivery.mmd</code>,
    outside your content folder, then run:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    code={`npx -y -p @mermaid-js/mermaid-cli@12.0.0 mmdc -i diagrams/delivery.mmd -o docs/images/delivery.svg`}
    lang="bash"
  />
  <p>
    Replace the fence with{" "}
    <code>![How a message moves from your app to a phone](./images/delivery.svg)</code>.
    The image doesn't follow the site's dark theme the way a live diagram
    does, so save this for diagrams readers need offline. The CLI also draws
    with Mermaid's own defaults, not Blume's dagre layout and classic look; to
    match the site, start the <code>.mmd</code> file with a front matter block
    that sets <code>layout: dagre</code> and <code>look: classic</code> under{" "}
    <code>config:</code>. The{" "}
    <a href="/guides/architecture-documentation-mermaid">Mermaid guide</a>{" "}
    covers writing diagrams in the first place.
  </p>

  <h3>Keep must-read content out of collapsed places</h3>
  <p>
    An accordion prints only its title unless the reader opened it before
    printing. Use accordions for optional detail, and keep a step or a
    required value where it prints. <code>expandable</code> code blocks don't
    have this problem: they print in full.
  </p>

  <h3>Host images next to the page</h3>
  <p>
    Reference images by relative path, as the test page does, and give each
    one alt text. The EPUB export downloads every image on the page, and a
    browser can only download an image from another host when that host
    allows it with CORS headers. An image it can't download is left out of the
    book.
  </p>

  <h3>Tidy the printout with theme.css</h3>
  <p>
    Blume's print stylesheet already removes the page chrome and opens{" "}
    <code>expandable</code> blocks. What it leaves alone is long lines, which
    print cut off. To wrap them on paper only, add a print rule to a{" "}
    <a href="/docs/configuration/theming#themecss"><code>theme.css</code></a>{" "}
    in your project root:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    code={`@media print {
  .prose pre,
  .prose pre code {
    white-space: pre-wrap !important;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
}`}
    lang="css"
    title="theme.css"
  />
  <Callout type="warning">
    These selectors target Blume's markup, not a documented styling API, so
    they can change in an update. Export the test page again after you upgrade
    Blume. They also only affect PDF, since the EPUB carries none of your CSS.
  </Callout>

  <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>
  <h3>There's no Export action</h3>
  <p>
    Check that <code>export</code> is set in <code>blume.config.ts</code> and
    that the page uses the default layout mode. In a window narrower than
    1,280 pixels, open the <strong>On this page</strong> menu above the
    content: the action is at the bottom of it. API operation pages never show
    it.
  </p>
  <h3>An image from another host is missing from the EPUB</h3>
  <p>
    Open the browser console. A warning that starts with{" "}
    <code>[blume] EPUB export left out an image it couldn't fetch</code> names
    the image the browser couldn't download, usually one on another host
    without CORS headers. The rest of the book still exports. Download the
    image into your content folder and reference it by relative path.
  </p>
  <h3>An image is missing from an EPUB made in dev</h3>
  <p>
    In <code>blume dev</code>, optimized images come from Astro's image
    endpoint, a URL with no file extension, and the EPUB records them without
    a media type. Do your final EPUB check on a production build:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    code={`npx blume build
npx blume preview`}
    lang="bash"
  />
  <h3>A diagram prints in dark colors</h3>
  <p>
    The page prints in the light theme, but a Mermaid diagram redraws itself
    for a theme change in the background, and a print that starts before the
    redraw finishes keeps the dark drawing. Switch the site to the light
    theme, wait for the diagram to redraw, and export again.
  </p>
  <h3>A diagram is blank in the PDF</h3>
  <p>
    It was printed before Mermaid finished drawing it. Wait until the diagram
    shows on screen, then print again.
  </p>
</GuidePage>
